<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Egerton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Walter Egerton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Walter Egerton was a British colonial administrator. Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> from 1904, he became governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> when the two were merged in 1906, with Lagos as capital, and promoted railway and harbour building. He was later governor of British Guiana from 1912 to 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-egerton.jpg" alt="Walter Egerton" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walter_Egerton.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Egerton was the only son of Walter Egerton, a gentleman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, and was educated at Tonbridge School. In 1880 he went out to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a> as a cadet and served there and in the protected states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> for more than twenty years, as a magistrate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and later as Collector and First Magistrate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>. He was called to the bar at the Middle Temple in 1896 and acted as Colonial Secretary of the Straits Settlements from 1899 to 1901.</p><p>As Resident of Negeri Sembilan in 1902–03 he dealt with a form of servitude under which a woman's illegitimate children were given into the custody of the local ruler. He decided that this was contrary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law and that the children belonged to their mothers, a view the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Perak supported.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> he took up the extension of the Lagos–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a> railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Oshogbo</a>, approved in November 1904 and reaching the town in April 1907, and he pressed for a further line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>. In August 1904 he was also made High Commissioner of the Southern Nigeria Protectorate, and when the two territories were united on 28 February 1906 the old Lagos Colony became the Western Province of the new colony. In the south-east, much of which was still outside British control, he sent out annual pacification patrols that usually obtained submission by the threat of force.</p><p>Egerton believed in deficit financing during a colony's growth and struggled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> over his budgets. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Agriculture was set up in 1910, and he encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> plantations, which he knew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. His administration also brought in policies tending to segregate Europeans and Africans, such as excluding Africans from the West African Medical Service, though he did not always approve of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> replaced him, with a mandate to unite Southern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>. The posting to British Guiana that followed was a demotion, possibly connected with his quarrels with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> officials. There he planned a railway of about 340 miles from the coast to the Brazilian border to open gold and diamond fields. He married Ada Maud Sneyd-Kynnersley in 1905; she died in 1934, and he died on 22 March 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Egerton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1906 it merged with the Lagos Colony, and Lagos became its capital under Governor <b>Walter Egerton</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a></li></ul></section>
